From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Grische <github@grische.xyz>
Date: Thu, 20 Aug 2026 17:39:03 +0200
Subject: MIPS: dts: intel-mips: widen the FRITZ!Box 7560 to its full 256 MiB

The memory node declared 128 MiB of a 256 MiB board, and both halves of
the reason given for it were wrong. The comment conceded that the chip
holds 256 MiB but claimed AVM's 4Kec bootcore lives in "the upper
region", so only the bottom half was safe to hand the InterAptiv kernel.
The bootcore owns the top 32 MiB, not the upper 128, and the 128 MiB
figure did not come from a measurement of this board at all -- it came
from matching an earlier work-in-progress device tree of our own. The
part is 256 MiB, and the vendor kernel for this board runs out of
0x28000000.

Declare the part, ending at exactly PHYS_OFFSET + SZ_256M = 0x30000000.
That address is two things at once here: the top of the fitted DRAM and
the top of the uncached window. On the FRITZ!Box 7590, whose part is
twice the size, only the second holds and the same number is a cap that
costs half the board; here the two agree, so the node is full and
bounded by the same edge. Nothing else has to move to honour the window.
The generic check in arch/mips/mm/dma-noncoherent.c that enforces it is
written against PHYS_OFFSET + SZ_256M rather than against a RAM size, so
at this width the node ends exactly where the check does and satisfies
it by construction.

Carve out the bootcore's RAM window and its mailbox pool in the shape
the FRITZ!Box 7590 already uses: no-map, cpunet@2dfc0000 and
bootcore@2e000000. They stop being free at this width. While the node
ended at 0x28000000 both ranges sat outside memblock.memory entirely and
their nomap was a supported no-op; now the 32 MiB window and the 256 KiB
pool come off usable RAM for real. That is worth paying. This DTB boots
single-kernel, where no bootcore runs and neither region is touched, but
the reservations are what make it survivable if one is ever started
against the blob -- and the bootcore image loads at 0x8dfffffc, which is
inside the cpunet pool rather than merely next to it. Delete them
together with the dual-kernel chain, not before it.

The FRITZ!Box 7590's third reservation, the shm RNG seed at 0x3dfb0000, is
deliberately not copied over: it sits above the uncached ceiling, so it
is outside this node at any width this board can be given.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-fable-5
Signed-off-by: Grische <github@grische.xyz>

diff --git a/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/seale_avm_fritz7560.dts b/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/seale_avm_fritz7560.dts
index abbf456943cd..a57763836c1f 100644
--- a/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/seale_avm_fritz7560.dts
+++ b/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/seale_avm_fritz7560.dts
@@ -29,15 +29,14 @@ avm-hw-revision {
 	};
 
 	/*
-	 * 128 MiB DDR at PHYS_OFFSET 0x20000000. Match the 5.10 reference
-	 * DTS for this board. The chip has 256 MiB physical but the 4Kec
-	 * bootcore lives in the upper region;
-	 * conservative Phase 1 exposes only the bottom 128 MiB to the
-	 * InterAptiv kernel.
+	 * 256 MiB DDR at PHYS_OFFSET, the full part. It ends at the top of
+	 * the uncached window (PHYS_OFFSET + SZ_256M): above that address the
+	 * KSEG1 alias of a page is a register address, so no coherent
+	 * allocation may land there.
 	 */
 	memory@20000000 {
 		device_type = "memory";
-		reg = <0x20000000 0x08000000>;
+		reg = <0x20000000 0x10000000>;
 	};
 
 	aliases {
@@ -69,6 +68,20 @@ ramoops@23700000 {
 			console-size = <0x80000>;
 			mem-type = <1>;
 		};
+
+		/*
+		 * Owned by the second CPU's boot loader; no-map so this
+		 * kernel never maps another CPU's memory.
+		 */
+		cpunet@2dfc0000 {
+			reg = <0x2dfc0000 0x40000>;
+			no-map;
+		};
+
+		bootcore@2e000000 {
+			reg = <0x2e000000 0x2000000>;
+			no-map;
+		};
 	};
 
 	/*
